```

Use plain semantic HTML for the body content inside each page (`<h2>`, `<p>`, `<ul>`) styled via a `prose`-like set of Tailwind utility classes applied at the `LegalPageLayout` level (font sizes, spacing, list markers) — don't reach for `@tailwindcss/typography` as a new dependency, just write the handful of utility classes directly since the content structure is simple and fixed.

### 3.2 Terms of Service (`app/legal/terms/page.tsx`)

Real sections, each a couple of paragraphs: what AETHER is (a tool for building, testing, and paper-trading algorithmic strategies — not a broker, not an advisor, not a fund manager); account eligibility and responsibilities; description of the credits/subscription model (consistent with `PLANS` and `CREDIT_BUNDLES` in `mock/data.ts` — credits don't expire, subscriptions unlock component tiers, etc.); the marketplace (publishing, forking, the 80% revenue share for Pro creators mentioned in `PLAN_COMPARISON`); acceptable use (no reverse engineering, no abuse of the platform to redistribute paid presets outside the marketplace); intellectual property (users retain rights to strategies they build; AETHER retains rights to the platform itself); disclaimers of warranty; limitation of liability; termination; governing law (India, consistent with the ₹ pricing and NSE/Nifty-focused product); how to contact for legal questions.

### 3.3 Privacy Policy (`app/legal/privacy/page.tsx`)

What data is collected (account info, trading strategies/bot graphs, usage analytics, payment info handled by the payment processor rather than stored directly); how it's used (running the product, improving it, nothing about selling data to third parties — be explicit that strategy data is never used to trade against users or sold to other users without explicit publishing consent, since that's a real trust concern for a platform where people build proprietary trading logic); data retention; how the existing "Export all data" feature (already referenced in `mock/data.ts`'s `FAQS`) fits in; cookies/analytics; children's privacy (not directed at anyone under 18, consistent with it being a trading product); user rights (access, correction, deletion); how to contact for privacy questions.

### 3.4 Risk Disclosure (`app/legal/risk-disclosure/page.tsx`)

This is the most important of the four — write it with real care, not filler. Cover: trading involves substantial risk of loss and is not suitable for everyone; past performance (including backtest results) does not guarantee future results — this exact framing already appears verbatim in `mock/data.ts`'s `quickstart` doc section, reuse that framing for consistency; backtests are simulations and have inherent limitations (survivorship bias, lookahead bias, overfitting — these are literally already named and explained in `DOC_SECTIONS`'s `backtesting` section, pull from there so the product is internally consistent rather than saying subtly different things in two places); paper trading does not reflect real market conditions like slippage under stress, liquidity gaps, or emotional decision-making with real capital at stake; AETHER is not a registered investment advisor and nothing in the product constitutes investment advice or a recommendation to buy or sell any security (this exact stance already appears in the `FAQS` — reuse it); users are solely responsible for their own trading decisions; automated/algorithmic strategies can fail in unexpected ways (bugs, bad data, market regime changes); real-money live trading is a separate, explicitly opt-in tier requiring additional verification, and users should only risk capital they can afford to lose.

### 3.5 Refund Policy (`app/legal/refund-policy/page.tsx`)

Cover: subscription refunds (e.g., pro-rated or not within some window — pick a reasonable, clearly-stated policy, such as "refundable within 7 days of a new subscription or upgrade if you haven't run more than a handful of backtests, at our discretion" — be specific and concrete rather than vague); credit purchases (typically non-refundable once any credits have been spent, since they unlock permanent component access — consistent with credits "never expiring" per `PLANS`); marketplace preset purchases (a short satisfaction window, consistent with a digital-goods marketplace); how to actually request a refund (a real-feeling process description, e.g. "contact support with your invoice ID" — reference the existing `Invoice` id format from `mock/data.ts`, like `INV-2026-0814`, so it feels internally consistent); processing time.

